Branch A: Define by structural/compositional properties (hypothetical, as specific compositional details may not be universally known or agreed upon):

Let's assume that we have a set of properties common to all rhizomes (Rhizoma), a set of properties common to all Curcuma species (Curcuma), and a set of properties unique to Curcuma xanthorrhiza (Cx).

In FOL:

Rhizoma = {r | r has a root system, r has underground stems}
Curcuma = {c | c belongs to the ginger family, c has yellow or white rhizomes}
Cx = {cx | cx has a specific chemical composition (e.g., curcuminoids, volatile oils), cx is native to Southeast Asia}

Curcuma xanthorrhiza(x) ↔ (x ∈ Curcuma ∧ x ∈ Rhizoma ∧ x ∈ Cx)

Branch B: Define by functional/relational properties (hypothetical, as specific functional properties may not be universally known or agreed upon):

Let's assume that we have a set of properties related to the medicinal uses (MedicalUse) and aroma (Aroma) of rhizomes.

In FOL:

MedicalUse = {mu | mu has anti-inflammatory properties, mu has antioxidant properties}
Aroma = {a | a has a distinctive aroma, a is used in perfumery}

Curcuma xanthorrhiza(x) ↔ (x has properties in MedicalUse ∧ x has properties in Aroma)

Branch C: Define by extensional examples and counter-examples (hypothetical, as the boundaries of Curcuma xanthorrhiza may not be clearly defined in all cases):

Let's assume that we have a set of Curcuma xanthorrhiza instances (Instances) and a set of non-Curcuma xanthorrhiza instances (NonInstances).

In FOL:

Instances = {cx1, cx2, ..., cxn}
NonInstances = {nx1, nx2, ..., nxm}

Curcuma xanthorrhiza(x) ↔ (x ∈ Instances ∧ ¬(x ∈ NonInstances))

Synthesis:

The best elements to synthesize into a single FOL biconditional would be a combination of Branch A and Branch C, as they provide a more comprehensive definition that includes both the structural/compositional properties and the extensional examples.

Curcuma xanthorrhiza(x) ↔ (x ∈ Curcuma ∧ x ∈ Rhizoma ∧ x ∈ Cx ∧ x ∈ Instances)

This definition states that an entity x is Curcuma xanthorrhiza if and only if it belongs to the Curcuma species, has the properties of a rhizome, has the unique properties of Curcuma xanthorrhiza, and is an instance of Curcuma xanthorrhiza.

∀x: Circular_Sulcus(x)
  ↛ (∃y: Line(Segment(y, x)) ∧
     ¬∃z: Point(z) (Bounded_In_Segment(z, y, x))
   ) ∨
  (∃y: Line(Segment(y, x)) ∧
    ∃z: Point(z) 
      (Is_Tangential_Point(z, y, x)
       ∧ ∀w: Point(w) ¬(Is_Collinear(Point(z, w), Segment(y, x)))
     )
  )

